WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.910 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  where are you from  chicago  no  where are you really from  the south side of chicago  no like  like like what are you  for years  i couldn't explain why incidents like  this made me feel so uncomfortable  you speak such good english  you must be good at math  your people's food is so spicy  innocuous  on the surface  but when repeated 10  20  50 times  i would start to get annoyed  but  these were just curious people with good  intentions right what right did i have  to be annoyed  it wasn't until i was in college  that i realized what i was experiencing  were racial microaggressions  psychologist gerald wing sue  defines microaggressions as brief  everyday exchanges that send denigrating  messages to certain individuals based on  their group membership  while they can be the product of  environmental or behavioral slights  most of the time  they are unintentional  and they happen all the time  in fact in a recent survey that i sent  out to my gsb classmates 77  of respondents identified as having been  the victim of a microaggression  so why do we care well take this example  when a young woman repeatedly hears  you have a great shot at that promotion  because they need a female on their  leadership team  she starts to feel like she doesn't  deserve to be there  based on her own merits alone  she starts to feel like she doesn't  belong  think that was too extreme of an example  take my opening question  no where are you really from  implies that  chicago cannot be a valid answer  i cannot be american  i must be a foreigner i cannot claim to  be from the place that i'm from  because of how i look  and i perpetually don't belong  now  the purpose of my talk today is not to  play the victim or  to even fantasize about a perfect world  where there are no microaggressions that  would be unrealistic because of all of  the unique backgrounds experiences and  implicit biases that everybody on this  earth carries with us me included  instead  i want to recognize  that these tiny snubs exist  all of us commit them and that's going  to happen  while our intent is not necessarily  malicious  different comments land with different  people in different ways  and i'm here  to bring awareness to that fact of life  and more importantly  to tell you what i've learned we can do  if and when  we find ourselves on either end of a  microaggression  a couple years ago i had a boss  casually throw me the advice to act more  like the men in the room  i was stunned  i had no words to express how i was  feeling  so i just said okay  and walked back to my desk  six months passed  and i heard those words haunting me  every single day  you should act more like a man  being a woman is not good enough  my sense of self-worth  my confidence and my productivity took a  hit  as i desperately tried to figure out how  to make sense of that comment  how could we have avoided this situation  let's turn to the receiving end there  are three things that you can do when  you find yourself the recipient of a  microaggression  first  and most importantly  prioritize yourself  there are situations where it doesn't  make sense to respond if i had gotten  this comment from a stranger i'd never  see again or  perhaps if my safety could have been  compromised if i did respond i would  give myself the permission to not say  anything  and i know  that it's unfair that the emotional  burden of whether or not to respond  falls on the recipient  but the more comfortable we get  with drawing boundaries and predicting  the implications to our lives if we do  not respond  the easier it gets  second  if you're in a place too  ask clarifying questions  i could have simply asked my boss  what exactly do you mean by that  he likely would have  said something about how i should be  more confident or how i should be more  aggressive with my good opinions  no mal intent  great  and third  if you have the emotional bandwidth too  state how the comment  impacted you  in one of the gsb's flagship classes on  interpersonal dynamics we always talk  about how  i cannot assume your intent since that's  something only you know  and that you  cannot assume the impact that your words  have on me since that's something only i  know  in the class  we call these assumptions crossing the  net  in the case of a microaggression  if it is productive to state how the  comment made you feel  it can help the perpetrator learn and  perhaps change their behavior for next  time  in my example  i could have simply told my boss  when you tell me to act like a man  i feel frustrated and dejected  because i can never be enough due to who  i am  i did eventually confront my boss and  tell him this and i remember he looked  shocked  he fumbled and  admitted that he hadn't realized that's  how i had taken it and that his intent  was just to be helpful  he did apologize for the effect that his  advice had on me  and i immediately felt like a part of  the team again  let's quickly turn  to the giving end  a couple years ago i was joking with my  friends in seattle about our experiences  living there when my friend made a  comment about the lack of diversity  there to which i said  what do you care you're just a straight  white man  i watched his face fall  and immediately knew  that i had crossed a line that i hadn't  meant to  being the perpetrator of  more than a few of these kinds of  careless comments has taught me three  things that you can do  when you find that you've just committed  a microaggression  first  accept the other person's truth  if they tell you  how you made them feel  don't cross the net  trust their reality and use it to inform  how you show up next time  which leads me to number two  be open to change and learn from the  experience  not only can that help you do better  next time  but that can help you become a better  ally if you see similar microaggressions  being committed by others  and third  if you find yourself feeling guilty  remember  we are only human  everybody makes mistakes  microaggressions are largely  unintentional and their impact is not  even uniform across members of the same  group  different comments land with different  people in different ways and while it's  more than okay to be curious about where  i'm from  the decision to reject my truth  turns ignorance into malice  and small pinches  into a bruise  it would be naive  to think that we can completely rid the  world of microaggressions but we can  react to them in a more productive way  by strengthening our muscles of asking  the questions and trusting the responses  it is my hope  that we are all better equipped  to handle this world's inevitable  imperfect situations with greater  empathy and trust  so that nobody feels like they don't  belong  thank you  [Music]

